% ============================================================================
% syllable-segmentation — breaking a word into its two syllables, a TWELFTH
% literacy sub-skill in the ADJ stdlib (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `syllable-count.adj` (which recalls only
% HOW MANY syllables a word has): a new `syllable_segmentation(word,
% syllable_one, syllable_two)` table names the actual TWO PARTS each word
% breaks into, not just their count. Discovered via a header-revisit:
% `syllable-count.adj`'s own header already quotes, verbatim, the exact
% syllable split for every one of its four words ("I say each syllable and
% put down a card: 'pea' [place a card] 'nut' [place a card]", etc.) — but
% that table's `syllable_count(word, count)` schema has no room for the
% actual syllable text, only the count. Since the split IS cleanly grounded
% in the SAME already-cited sentences, it earns its own sibling table with a
% DIFFERENT schema, the same "sibling table, different schema" shape already
% used for `comet-part.adj`/`comet-tail-type.adj`, `lung-lobes.adj`/
% `lung-lobe-names.adj`, and `chemical-bonds.adj`/`chemical-bond-family.adj`.
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? syllable_segmentation(peanut, $S1, $S2)   % pea, nut
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a THREE-column table like
% `phoneme-segmentation.adj`): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `syllable_segmentation(word, syllable_one, syllable_two)` carrying the
% Reading Rockets citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding
% query — the engine returns the two syllables WITH their source, on the
% CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on any word not one of the four
% the cited page's card-placement demonstration walks through. The query
% also runs BACKWARD — bind the two syllables and recall the word:
%
%     ? syllable_segmentation($Word, pea, nut)   % peanut
%
% The four segmentations this table carries, as a little truth table (the
% SAME four words `syllable-count.adj` already tables, this time carrying
% their actual parts instead of just a count):
%
%     word     syllable_one   syllable_two
%     ------   ------------   ------------
%     peanut   pea            nut
%     pencil   pen            cil
%     sunset   sun            set
%     laptop   lap            top
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Reading Rockets' "Phonological and
% Phonemic Awareness: In Practice" module — the SAME already-vetted page,
% and the exact SAME four verbatim spans `syllable-count.adj`'s own header
% already quotes in full (re-used here rather than re-typed, since they are
% the same cited sentences grounding a different axis of the same source
% material) — WebFetch re-verified TWICE more this cycle for consistency,
% both byte-identical to `syllable-count.adj`'s existing quotes:
%
%   peanut -> pea, nut
%     "I say the whole word: 'Peanut'. I say each syllable and put down a
%      card: 'pea' [place a card] 'nut' [place a card]."
%   pencil -> pen, cil
%     "The word is: 'pencil'. 'Pen' 'cil' [place a card for each syllable]."
%   sunset -> sun, set
%     "I say the whole word: 'sunset'. I say each syllable and put down a
%      card: 'sun' [place a card] 'set' [place a card]."
%   laptop -> lap, top
%     "I say each syllable and put down a card: 'lap' [place a card] 'top'
%      [place a card]."
%
% `trust consensus` — the same tier `syllable-count.adj` already uses for
% this same page. Deliberately scoped to ONLY these four segmentations the
% cited page walks through step by step — honestly narrow (mirroring
% `syllable-count.adj`'s own precedent) rather than inventing a segmentation
% the source does not demonstrate. (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table syllable_segmentation {
    columns word, syllable_one, syllable_two

    row (peanut, pea, nut)
    row (pencil, pen, cil)
    row (sunset, sun, set)
    row (laptop, lap, top)

    source "I say the whole word: 'Peanut'. I say each syllable and put down a card: 'pea' [place a card] 'nut' [place a card]."
    locator "https://www.readingrockets.org/reading-101/reading-101-learning-modules/course-modules/phonological-and-phonemic-awareness/practice"
    trust consensus
}
